--TEST--
A decompression that would pass either half of its bound throws rather than truncating, and the refusal names the rule — `rule:core-classes/decompression-bound`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// 16 KiB of one octet: every codec here takes it to well under a hundred
// octets, which is the shape a decompression bomb has — small on the wire and
// large in the heap. `Core\Compress` is Tier 0 because that is policy, and a
// policy a program can decline is not one.
bytes $payload = Core\Str::repeat("A", 16384) as bytes;
bytes $frame = Core\Compress::compress($payload, Core\Codec::Gzip);

// The absolute half, asserted on both sides of the boundary. One octet short of
// the output refuses; the output's own size is accepted whole. A member that
// stopped one entry early would print plausibly against either half alone.
try {
    Core\Compress::decompress($frame, Core\Codec::Gzip, 16383);
    echo "the bound did not hold\n";
} catch (ParseError $bomb) {
    echo $bomb->message, "\n";
}
echo "at the bound: ",
    Core\Bytes::length(Core\Compress::decompress($frame, Core\Codec::Gzip, 16384)),
    "\n";

// The ratio half, with the absolute half wide open: a bomb is small on the
// wire, so a byte ceiling alone is one a small request still reaches.
try {
    Core\Compress::decompress($frame, Core\Codec::Gzip, 1048576, 2);
    echo "the ratio did not hold\n";
} catch (ParseError $ratio) {
    echo "ratio refused\n";
}

// And what is refused is refused whole. Nothing here answers a prefix: a
// truncated decompression that looks like success is the bug this class exists
// to prevent, so the caller never gets a shorter answer than it asked about.
try {
    bytes $partial = Core\Compress::decompress($frame, Core\Codec::Gzip, 1024);
    echo "a bounded-out decompression answers: ", Core\Bytes::length($partial), " octets\n";
} catch (ParseError $nothing) {
    echo "a bounded-out decompression answers: nothing at all\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Compress::decompress(): a `Core\Codec::Gzip` frame decompressing past the 16383 octet ceiling is refused rather than truncated (rule:core-classes/decompression-bound). `[limits] max_decompressed` and `[limits] max_decompression_ratio` are the operator's ceiling, which a call naming `$maxBytes` or `$maxRatio` lowers and nothing raises.
at the bound: 16384
ratio refused
a bounded-out decompression answers: nothing at all
